[[en:draft:Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko]]
==Overview==
The title of Kii no kuni no miyatsuko refers to the role of a provincial [[governor]] with ritual duties in ancient [[Kii Province]], Japan. This position, known as "[[kuni no miyatsuko]]," is mentioned in historic texts such as the [[Kojiki]] and [[Nihon Shoki]]. The characters used to write the term in the [[Nihon Shoki]] were adopted under the [[Ritsuryō system]], a legal system in ancient Japan.<ref name="mw-2023a" />



The system of kuni no miyatsuko includes the chieftain of the Nagusa district, Nagusahiko, who was integrated into the imperial control structure in the early phases of [[kofun]] construction in the fifth century. Some theories suggest the kuni no miyatsuko system was established in the [[6th century|sixth century]].<ref name="mw-2023a" />

Following the [[Taika Reform]], which aimed to consolidate imperial power and regulate land ownership in [[645]], the kuni no miyatsuko also served as gunji, or district administrators. This dual role became less common after the [[Nara period]].<ref name="mw-2023a" />

The {{Interlanguage link|Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko|ja|紀伊国造}} ran the [[Hinokuma Shrine|Hinokuma and Kunikakasu shrines]] and their current descendants serve as gūji, or chief priests. The Jōgan Gishiki, a document from the period, outlines the ritual for appointing the {{Interlanguage link|Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko|ja|紀伊国造}} who served these shrines.<ref name="mw-2023a" />

==Famous members==

