[[en:draft:Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko]]
==Overview==
The Kinoatai family held a title called "Kuni no Miyatsuko" of the [[Kii Province]]. The title meant they were a provincial governor with both religious and secular duties. The title is mentioned in historic texts such as the [[Kojiki|Kojiki]] and [[Nihon Shoki|Nihon Shoki]]. The Chinese characters used to write the term in the [[Nihon Shoki|Nihon Shoki]] were adopted under the [[Ritsuryō system|Ritsuryō system]], a legal system in ancient Japan, and they can also be read as Kokuzo.<ref name="mw-2023a" />

The system of kuni no miyatsuko includes the chieftain of the Nagusa district, Nagusahiko, who was integrated into the imperial control structure in the early phases of [[kofun]] construction in the fifth century. Some theories suggest the kuni no miyatsuko system was established in the [[6th century|sixth century]].<ref name="mw-2023a" />

Following the [[Taika Reform|Taika Reform]], which aimed to consolidate imperial power and regulate land ownership in [[645]], the kuni no miyatsuko also served as gunji, or district administrators. This dual role became less common after the [[Nara period|Nara period]].<ref name="mw-2023a" />

The {{Interlanguage link|Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko|ja|紀伊国造|simple|User:Immanuelle/Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko|fr}} ran the [[Hinokuma Shrine|Hinokuma and Kunikakasu shrines]] and their current descendants serve as gūji, or chief priests. The Jōgan Gishiki, a document from the period, outlines the ritual for appointing the {{Interlanguage link|Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko|ja|紀伊国造|simple|User:Immanuelle/Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko}} who served these shrines.<ref name="mw-2023a" />

