* The first historically verifiable Miyatsuko is likely {{ill|Ki no Oshikatsu|ja|紀忍勝}}, mentioned in the {{ill|Nihon Shoki|ja|日本書紀}} (entries for Emperor Bidatsu's 12th year, 7th and 10th months).<ref>{{Cite book|和書|title=古代氏族の系譜を読み解く|date=2022年2月1日|publisher=吉川弘文館|page=134|author=鈴木正信}}</ref>

=== Clan Lineage ===
The Ki clan (紀氏) originally held the {{ill|Kimi|ja|君 (姓)}} title and later transitioned to {{ill|Natori|ja|直 (姓)}} in the {{ill|Kōfu Nenki|ja|庚午年籍}}. By 835 CE, they received the {{ill|Shinni|ja|宿禰}} title.<ref>[[宝賀寿男]]「第1章 天神系氏族　第6節 紀国造氏族　1紀国造紀直、紀宿祢（一）」『古代氏族系譜集成』中巻、古代氏族研究会、879-881頁。</ref>

The Ki clan served as priests of Hinokuma and Kunikakasu shrines for over two millennia. The preservation of such a lineage, aside from the Imperial Family, is rare. Comparable examples include the {{ill|Izumo Miyatsuko|ja|出雲国造}} lineages of the Senge and Kitajima families and priestly clans such as the {{ill|Aso Shrine|ja|阿蘇神社}}'s Aso family or the {{ill|Sumiyoshi Shrine|ja|住吉大社}}'s Tsumori family.<ref>『[[Jōgan Gishiki|貞観儀式]]』巻第10。</ref>

== Historical Evolution ==
The Miyatsuko family adapted through Japan’s tumultuous history. In the {{ill|Edo period|ja|江戸時代}}, they maintained regional influence through alliances with the {{ill|Kii Domain|ja|紀州藩}} and others. Despite the {{ill|Sengoku period|ja|戦国時代}}’s challenges, including the {{ill|Siege of Kii Province|ja|紀州征伐}}, the clan continued to serve as priests and feudal administrators.

=== Transition to Modern Era ===
In the {{ill|Meiji period|ja|明治時代}}, the family entered the {{ill|Kazoku|ja|華族}} peerage system, obtaining baronial status. Members served in government roles, such as {{ill|Baron Ki Toshihide|ja|紀俊秀}}, who represented the family in the {{ill|House of Peers|ja|貴族院}}.<ref>{{Cite book|和書|date=2006年（平成18年）|title=華族 近代日本貴族の虚像と実像|author=小田部雄次|authorlink=小田部雄次|publisher=[[中央公論新社]]|series=[[中公新書]]1836|isbn= 978-4121018366|ref=harv}}</ref>

