{{Interlanguage link|Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko|ja|紀伊国造|simple|User:Immanuelle/Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko|fr}} is a [[Kuni no miyatsuko|Kuni no miyatsuko]] that had a uniquely high amount of power similar to the {{Ill|Izumo clan|en||simple|User:Immanuelle/Izumo clan}}.<ref name="mw-2023a">{{Cite web |date=2023-03-10 |title=Kuni no miyatsuko {{!}} 國學院大學デジタルミュージアム |url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=8839 |access-date=2023-12-02 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230310081317/https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=8839 |archive-date=2023-03-10 }}</ref>


The Kinoatai family was responsible for this office. According to the Sendai Kuji Hongi, a historic record, Amenomichine no Mikoto, a descendant of Kami Musubi no Mikoto, was the first to be appointed as Ki no Kuni no Miyatsuko during the reign of Emperor Jinmu. However, Ōnagusahiko is often considered the actual precursor of the {{Interlanguage link|Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko|ja|紀伊国造|simple|User:Immanuelle/Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko }}.<ref name="mw-2023a" />

They run [[Hinokuma Shrine|Hinokuma Shrine]].<ref name="mw-2023a" /><ref name="web.archive.org-2023">{{Cite web|date=2023-12-16|title=Kinokuninomiyatsuko {{!}} 國學院大學デジタルミュージアム|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231216060822/https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=8597|access-date=2023-12-16|website=web.archive.org}}</ref> and are the Kuni no miyatsuko of [[Kii Province|Kii Province]].

They descend from {{ill|Amanomichine|ja|天道根命|en|6=simple|7=User:Immanuelle/Amanomichine|8=fr}}<ref name="web.archive.org-2023" />.

They are believed to be buried at {{ill|Iwase-Senzuka Kofun Cluster|en||simple|User:Immanuelle/Iwase-Senzuka Kofun Cluster }}<ref name="Shiseki">{{cite book|title=(国指定史跡事典) National Historic Site Encyclopedia|last1=Isomura|first1=Yukio|last2=Sakai|first2=Hideya|date=2012|publisher=学生社|isbn=4311750404}}{{in lang|ja}}</ref>.<!-- citation taken from the article, see it for content -->.

They worshipped the daughters of Susanoo.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Encyclopedia of Shinto詳細|url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/id=9161|access-date=2024-04-01|website=國學院大學デジタルミュージアム|language=ja}}</ref>
[[ja: 紀伊国造]]
[[fr:{{PAGENAME}}]][[zh:{{PAGENAME}}]][[ja:{{PAGENAME}}]]
[[en:draft:Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko]]
==Overview==
The Kinoatai family held a title called "Kuni no Miyatsuko" of the [[Kii Province]]. The title meant they were a provincial governor with both religious and secular duties. The title is mentioned in historic texts such as the [[Kojiki|Kojiki]] and [[Nihon Shoki|Nihon Shoki]]. The Chinese characters used to write the term in the [[Nihon Shoki|Nihon Shoki]] were adopted under the [[Ritsuryō system|Ritsuryō system]], a legal system in ancient Japan, and they can also be read as Kokuzo.<ref name="mw-2023a" />

The system of kuni no miyatsuko includes the chieftain of the Nagusa district, Nagusahiko, who was integrated into the imperial control structure in the early phases of [[kofun]] construction in the fifth century. Some theories suggest the kuni no miyatsuko system was established in the [[6th century|sixth century]].<ref name="mw-2023a" />

Following the {{ill|Taika Reform|en}}, which aimed to consolidate imperial power and regulate land ownership in [[645]], the kuni no miyatsuko also served as gunji, or district administrators. This dual role became less common after the [[Nara period|Nara period]].<ref name="mw-2023a" />

The {{Interlanguage link|Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko|ja|紀伊国造|simple|User:Immanuelle/Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko|fr}} ran the [[Hinokuma Shrine|Hinokuma and Kunikakasu shrines]] and their current descendants serve as gūji, or chief priests. The Jōgan Gishiki, a document from the period, outlines the ritual for appointing the {{Interlanguage link|Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko|ja|紀伊国造|simple|User:Immanuelle/Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko}} who served these shrines.<ref name="mw-2023a" />

==Overview==


