'''Kinokuno Clan''' (紀氏, ''Ki no Uji'') is a clan whose family name is "Ki."

The Kinokuno Clan was an ancient {{ill|Gōzoku|ja|豪族}} based in {{ill|Kinori|ja|紀里}}, Heguri District, Yamato Province (present-day Kamisho, {{ill|Heguri|ja|平群町}}, Ikoma District, Nara Prefecture). Initially holding the title of {{ill|Omi|ja|臣}}, they were renamed {{ill|Asomi|ja|朝臣}} under the {{ill|Yatsuka no Kabane|ja|八色の姓}} system established in the 13th year of {{ill|Emperor Tenmu|ja|天武天皇}}'s reign (684 AD).

== Overview ==

According to sources like the {{ill|Nihon Shoki|ja|記紀}}, the Kinokuno Clan descended from {{ill|Emperor Kōgen|ja|孝元天皇}}, with {{ill|Ki no Tsuno|ja|紀角}}, a son of {{ill|Takenouchi no Sukune|ja|武内宿禰}}, as their progenitor. Both Ki no Tsuno and his predecessor were maternally descended from the {{ill|Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko|ja|紀伊国造}} family. This relationship suggests that the clan served the {{ill|Yamato Kingship|ja|ヤマト王権}} early on as a military house. Figures like {{ill|Ki no Oyu|ja|紀小弓}}, {{ill|Ki no Oiwashi|ja|紀大磐}}, and {{ill|Ki no Otomaro|ja|紀男麻呂}} were active in military and diplomatic affairs, particularly regarding the Korean Peninsula, during the reigns of {{ill|Emperor Yūryaku|ja|雄略天皇}}, {{ill|Emperor Kenzo|ja|顕宗天皇}}, and {{ill|Emperor Kinmei|ja|欽明天皇}}.

Although the Kinokuno Clan belonged to the Takenouchi lineage, like the {{ill|Katsuragi Clan|ja|葛城氏}}, {{ill|Kose Clan|ja|巨勢氏}}, and {{ill|Heguri Clan|ja|平群氏}}, they notably did not produce a {{ill|Ōomi|ja|大臣}} before the {{ill|Taika Reform|ja|大化の改新}} period.

During the reign of {{ill|Emperor Tenji|ja|天智天皇}}, {{ill|Ki no Ōtoko|ja|紀大人}} served as the {{ill|Ōtsukasa|ja|御史大夫}}. In the Nara period, {{ill|Ki no Maro|ja|紀麻呂}} became a {{ill|Dainagon|ja|大納言}}, {{ill|Ki no Masaru|ja|紀麻路}} a {{ill|Chūnagon|ja|中納言|1=Chūnagon}}, and {{ill|Ki no Iimaro|ja|紀飯麻呂}} a {{ill|Sangi|ja|参議}}. The clan flourished as an {{ill|Outer Clan|ja|外戚}} after {{ill|Prince Shirakabe|ja|白壁王}}, born to {{ill|Ki no Tsuji Hime|ja|紀橡姫}} and {{ill|Prince Shiki|ja|志貴皇子}}, ascended as {{ill|Emperor Kōnin|ja|光仁天皇}}. However, during the {{ill|Heian period|ja|平安時代}}, the clan's influence waned due to the rise of the {{ill|Fujiwara Hokke|ja|藤原北家}}.

By the early Heian period, only a few members, such as {{ill|Ki no Haseo|ja|紀長谷雄}}, managed to hold significant positions. They later transitioned to literary contributions, producing figures like {{ill|Ki no Tsurayuki|ja|紀貫之}}, {{ill|Ki no Tomonori|ja|紀友則}}, and {{ill|Ki no Tokifumi|ja|紀時文}}.

