'''Kinokuno Clan''' (紀氏, ''Ki no Uji'') is a clan whose family name is "Ki."

The Kinokuno Clan was an ancient {{ill|Gōzoku|ja|豪族|12=draft}} based in {{ill|Kinori|ja|紀里|12=draft}}, Heguri District, Yamato Province (present-day Kamisho, {{ill|Heguri|ja|平群町|12=draft}}, Ikoma District, Nara Prefecture). Initially holding the title of {{ill|Omi|ja|臣|12=draft}}, they were renamed {{ill|Asomi|ja|朝臣|12=draft}} under the {{ill|Yatsuka no Kabane|ja|八色の姓|1=Eight Kabane system|12=draft}} system established in the 13th year of {{ill|Emperor Tenmu|ja|Emperor Tenmu|1=Emperor Tenmu|12=draft}}'s reign (684 AD).

== Overview ==

According to sources like the {{ill|Nihon Shoki|ja|記紀|12=draft}}, the Kinokuno Clan descended from {{ill|Emperor Kōgen|ja|孝元天皇|12=draft}}, with {{ill|Ki no Tsuno|ja|紀角|12=draft}}, a son of {{ill|Takenouchi no Sukune|ja|武内宿禰|12=draft}}, as their progenitor. Both Ki no Tsuno and his predecessor were maternally descended from the {{ill|Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko|ja|紀氏|1=Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko|de|Ki (Klan)|uk|Рід Кі|ko|기씨 (황별)|fr|Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko|3=紀伊国造|12=draft}} family. This relationship suggests that the clan served the {{ill|Yamato Kingship|ja|ヤマト王権|12=draft}} early on as a military house. Figures like {{ill|Ki no Oyu|ja|紀小弓|12=draft}}, {{ill|Ki no Oiwashi|ja|紀大磐|12=draft}}, and {{ill|Ki no Otomaro|ja|紀男麻呂|12=draft}} were active in military and diplomatic affairs, particularly regarding the Korean Peninsula, during the reigns of {{ill|Emperor Yūryaku|ja|雄略天皇|12=draft}}, {{ill|Emperor Kenzo|ja|顕宗天皇|12=draft}}, and {{ill|Emperor Kinmei|ja|欽明天皇|12=draft}}.

Although the Kinokuno Clan belonged to the Takenouchi lineage, like the {{ill|Katsuragi Clan|ja|葛城氏|12=draft}}, {{ill|Kose Clan|ja|巨勢氏|12=draft}}, and {{ill|Heguri Clan|ja|平群氏|12=draft}}, they notably did not produce a {{ill|Ōomi|ja|大臣|12=draft}} before the {{ill|Taika Reform|ja|大化の改新|12=draft}} period.

During the reign of {{ill|Emperor Tenji|ja|天智天皇|12=draft}}, {{ill|Ki no Ōtoko|ja|紀大人|12=draft}} served as the {{ill|Ōtsukasa|ja|御史大夫|12=draft}}. In the Nara period, {{ill|Ki no Maro|ja|紀麻呂|12=draft}} became a {{ill|Dainagon|ja|大納言|12=draft}}, {{ill|Ki no Masaru|ja|紀麻路|12=draft}} a {{ill|Chūnagon|ja|中納言|1=Chūnagon|12=draft}}, and {{ill|Ki no Iimaro|ja|紀飯麻呂|12=draft}} a {{ill|Sangi|ja|参議|12=draft}}. The clan flourished as an {{ill|Outer Clan|ja|外戚|12=draft}} after {{ill|Prince Shirakabe|ja|白壁王|12=draft}}, born to {{ill|Ki no Tsuji Hime|ja|紀橡姫|12=draft}} and {{ill|Prince Shiki|ja|志貴皇子|12=draft}}, ascended as {{ill|Emperor Kōnin|ja|光仁天皇|12=draft}}. However, during the {{ill|Heian period|ja|平安時代|12=draft}}, the clan's influence waned due to the rise of the {{ill|Fujiwara Hokke|ja|藤原北家|12=draft}}.

By the early Heian period, only a few members, such as {{ill|Ki no Haseo|ja|紀長谷雄|12=draft}}, managed to hold significant positions. They later transitioned to literary contributions, producing figures like {{ill|Ki no Tsurayuki|ja|紀貫之|12=draft}}, {{ill|Ki no Tomonori|ja|紀友則|12=draft}}, and {{ill|Ki no Tokifumi|ja|紀時文|12=draft}}.

