'''Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko''' (紀伊国造) refers to the {{ill|Miyatsuko|ja|国造|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Miyatsuko|qq=}} who governed {{ill|Kii Province|ja|紀伊国|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kii Province|qq=}} (modern {{ill|Wakayama Prefecture|ja|和歌山県|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Wakayama Prefecture|qq=}}). This clan of Miyatsuko descended from the {{ill|Shinbetsu|ja|神別|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shinbetsu|qq=}}, specifically the '''Ki-shi''' (紀氏). Historically, they held the dual responsibilities of the office of Miyatsuko in Kii Province and the priestly duties of {{ill|Hinokuma Shrine|ja|日前神宮・國懸神宮|Hinokuma Shrine=Hinokuma Shrine|lt=Hinokuma Shrine and Kunikakasu Shrine|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Hinokuma Shrine|qq=}}. Even after the abolition of the Miyatsuko system during the establishment of the {{ill|Ritsuryō system|ja|律令制|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ritsuryō system|qq=}}, they retained the title "Miyatsuko" as hereditary priests (宮司) of these shrines.

== Overview ==

=== Name Variants ===
In the {{ill|Sendai Kuji Hongi|ja|先代旧事本紀|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sendai Kuji Hongi|qq=}}, the name is recorded as 紀伊国造 (Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko). Older texts use variants such as '''Ki no Kuni no Miyatsuko''' (木国造) in the {{ill|Kojiki|ja|古事記|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kojiki|qq=}} and '''Ki no Kuni no Miyatsuko''' (紀国造) in the {{ill|Nihon Shoki|ja|日本書紀|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Nihon Shoki|qq=}}.

=== Ancestry ===
* The {{ill|Sendai Kuji Hongi|ja|先代旧事本紀|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sendai Kuji Hongi|qq=}}'s "Miyatsuko Chronicle" attributes the establishment of Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko to {{ill|Amanomichine|ja|天道根命|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Amanomichine|qq=}}, a fifth-generation descendant of {{ill|Kamimusubi|ja|神皇産霊命|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kamimusubi|qq=}}, during the reign of {{ill|Emperor Jimmu|ja|神武天皇|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Emperor Jimmu|qq=}}. The {{ill|Kii Zoku Fudoki|ja|紀伊続風土記|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kii Zoku Fudoki|qq=}} concurs, stating that Ame no Michine no Mikoto was granted control of Kii Province after Emperor Jimmu’s unification of the Kinai region.
* The first historically verifiable Miyatsuko is likely {{ill|Ki no Oshikatsu|ja|紀忍勝|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ki no Oshikatsu|qq=}}, mentioned in the {{ill|Nihon Shoki|ja|日本書紀|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Nihon Shoki|qq=}} (entries for Emperor Bidatsu's 12th year, 7th and 10th months).<ref>{{Cite book|和書|title=古代氏族の系譜を読み解く|date=2022nd year 2月1日|publisher=吉川弘文館|page=134|author=鈴木正信}}</ref>

=== Clan Lineage ===
The Ki clan (紀氏) originally held the {{ill|Kimi|ja|君 (姓)|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kimi|qq=}} title and later transitioned to {{ill|Natori|ja|直 (姓)|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Natori|qq=}} in the {{ill|Kōfu Nenki|ja|庚午年籍|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kōfu Nenki|qq=}}. By 835 CE, they received the {{ill|Shinni|ja|宿禰|1=Sukune|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shinni|13=User:Immanuelle/Sukune|qq=}} title.<ref>{{ill|Toshio Hōga|ja|宝賀寿男|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Toshio Hōga|qq=}}「第1章 天神系氏族　第6節 紀国造氏族　1紀国造紀直、紀宿祢（一）」『古代氏族系譜集成』中巻、古代氏族研究会、879-881頁。</ref>

The Ki clan served as priests of Hinokuma and Kunikakasu shrines for over two millennia. The preservation of such a lineage, aside from the Imperial Family, is rare. Comparable examples include the {{ill|Izumo Miyatsuko|ja|Izumo Province 造|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Izumo Miyatsuko|qq=}} lineages of the Senge and Kitajima families and priestly clans such as the {{ill|Aso Shrine|ja|阿蘇神社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Aso Shrine|qq=}}'s Aso family or the {{ill|Sumiyoshi Shrine|ja|住吉大社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sumiyoshi Shrine|qq=}}'s Tsumori family.<ref>『[[Jōgan Gishiki|貞観儀式]]』巻第10。</ref>

== Historical Evolution ==
The Miyatsuko family adapted through Japan’s tumultuous history. In the {{ill|Edo period|ja|江戸時代|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Edo period|qq=}}, they maintained regional influence through alliances with the {{ill|Kii Domain|ja|紀州藩|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kii Domain|qq=}} and others. Despite the {{ill|Sengoku period|ja|戦国時代|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sengoku period|qq=}}’s challenges, including the {{ill|Siege of Kii Province|ja|紀州征伐|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Siege of Kii Province|qq=}}, the clan continued to serve as priests and feudal administrators.

=== Transition to Modern Era ===
In the {{ill|Meiji period|ja|明治時代|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Meiji period|qq=}}, the family entered the {{ill|Kazoku|ja|華族|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kazoku|qq=}} peerage system, obtaining baronial status. Members served in government roles, such as {{ill|Baron Ki Toshihide|ja|紀俊秀|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Baron Ki Toshihide|qq=}}, who represented the family in the {{ill|House of Peers|ja|貴族院|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/House of Peers|qq=}}.<ref>{{Cite book|和書|date=2006th year （平成18th year ）|title=華族 近代日本貴族の虚像と実像|author=小田部雄次|authorlink=小田部雄次|publisher=[[中央公論新社]]|series=[[中公新書]]1836|isbn= 978-4121018366|ref=harv}}</ref>

