'''Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko''' (紀伊国造) refers to the [[:en:Kuni no miyatsuko|Miyatsuko]] who governed [[:en:Kii Province|Kii Province]] (modern [[:en:Wakayama Prefecture|Wakayama Prefecture]]). This clan of Miyatsuko descended from the {{ill|Shinbetsu|ja|神別|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shinbetsu|qq=}}, specifically the '''Ki-shi''' (紀氏). Historically, they held the dual responsibilities of the office of Miyatsuko in Kii Province and the priestly duties of [[:en:Hinokuma Shrine|Hinokuma Shrine and Kunikakasu Shrine]]. Even after the abolition of the Miyatsuko system during the establishment of the [[:en:Ritsuryō|Ritsuryō system]], they retained the title "Miyatsuko" as hereditary priests (宮司) of these shrines.

== Overview ==

=== Name Variants ===
In the [[:en:Kujiki|Sendai Kuji Hongi]], the name is recorded as 紀伊国造 (Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko). Older texts use variants such as '''Ki no Kuni no Miyatsuko''' (木国造) in the [[:en:Kojiki|Kojiki]] and '''Ki no Kuni no Miyatsuko''' (紀国造) in the [[:en:Nihon Shoki|Nihon Shoki]].

=== Ancestry ===
* The [[:en:Kujiki|Sendai Kuji Hongi]]'s "Miyatsuko Chronicle" attributes the establishment of Kii no Kuni no Miyatsuko to {{ill|Amanomichine|ja|天道根命|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Amanomichine|qq=}}, a fifth-generation descendant of {{ill|Kamimusubi|ja|神皇産霊命|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kamimusubi|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to カミムスビ}}, during the reign of [[:en:Emperor Jimmu|Emperor Jimmu]]. The {{ill|Kii Zoku Fudoki|ja|紀伊続風土記|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kii Zoku Fudoki|qq=}} concurs, stating that Ame no Michine no Mikoto was granted control of Kii Province after Emperor Jimmu’s unification of the Kinai region.
* The first historically verifiable Miyatsuko is likely {{ill|Ki no Oshikatsu|ja|紀忍勝|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ki no Oshikatsu|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 紀押勝|1=ki no oshikatsu|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q58139743}}, mentioned in the [[:en:Nihon Shoki|Nihon Shoki]] (entries for Emperor Bidatsu's 12th year, 7th and 10th months).<ref>{{Cite book|和書|title=古代氏族の系譜を読み解く|date=2022nd year 2月1日|publisher=吉川弘文館|page=134|author=鈴木正信}}</ref>

=== Clan Lineage ===
The Ki clan (紀氏) originally held the {{ill|Kimi (kabane)|lt=Kimi (kabane)|lt=Kimi|ja|君 (姓)|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kimi|qq=}} title and later transitioned to {{ill|Natori|ja|直 (姓)|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Natori|qq=|1=Natori|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q55526687}} in the {{ill|Kōfu Nenki|ja|庚午年籍|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kōfu Nenki|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 古代日本の戸籍制度|1=koseki system in classical Japan|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q11410913}}. By 835 CE, they received the [[:en:Sukune|Sukune]] title.<ref>{{ill|Toshio Hōga|ja|宝賀寿男|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Toshio Hōga|qq=}}「第1章 天神系氏族　第6節 紀国造氏族　1紀国造紀直、紀宿祢（一）」『古代氏族系譜集成』中巻、古代氏族研究会、879-881頁。</ref>

The Ki clan served as priests of Hinokuma and Kunikakasu shrines for over two millennia. The preservation of such a lineage, aside from the Imperial Family, is rare. Comparable examples include the {{ill|Izumo Miyatsuko|ja|Izumo Province 造|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Izumo Miyatsuko|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}} lineages of the Senge and Kitajima families and priestly clans such as the [[:en:Aso Shrine|Aso Shrine]]'s Aso family or the [[:en:Sumiyoshi-taisha|Sumiyoshi Shrine]]'s Tsumori family.<ref>『[[Jōgan Gishiki|貞観儀式]]』巻第10。</ref>

== Historical Evolution ==
The Miyatsuko family adapted through Japan’s tumultuous history. In the [[:en:Edo period|Edo period]], they maintained regional influence through alliances with the [[:en:Kishū Domain|Kii Domain]] and others. Despite the {{ill|Sengoku period|ja|戦国時代|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sengoku period|qq=|1=Sengoku Period (Japan)|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q117415390}}’s challenges, including the {{ill|Siege of Kii Province|ja|紀州征伐|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Siege of Kii Province|qq=|1=Battle of Kishuu|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q10855721}}, the clan continued to serve as priests and feudal administrators.

=== Transition to Modern Era ===
In the [[:en:Meiji era|Meiji period]], the family entered the [[:en:Kazoku|Kazoku]] peerage system, obtaining baronial status. Members served in government roles, such as {{ill|Baron Ki Toshihide|ja|紀俊秀|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Baron Ki Toshihide|qq=|1=Toshihide Kii|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q11604973}}, who represented the family in the [[:en:Chamber of Peers|House of Peers]].<ref>{{Cite book|和書|date=2006th year （平成18th year ）|title=華族 近代日本貴族の虚像と実像|author=小田部雄次|authorlink=小田部雄次|publisher=[[中央公論新社]]|series=[[中公新書]]1836|isbn= 978-4121018366|ref=harv}}</ref>

